      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-11827",
        "knowledge_point_id": "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968",
        "knowledge_point": "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point",
        "problem": "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(3,-5)$ and which passes through the point $(0,4).$",
        "worked_solution": "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(3,-5).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y-(-5)) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(0,4),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+5) \\\\ (0-3)^{2} & =4p(4+5) \\\\ 9 & =36p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+5) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{4})(y+5) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =y+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
